Hi all,

Mark -----------------------------------------

I tried your approach again with nll += rather than nll -=. The model ran and converged and the results were identical to the penalty approach I used, both in terms of results and the spike in memory.

Hans -------------------------------------------

Yes, it's due to the large number of observations. When you thin the dataset to only 1000 samples the memory usage doesn't spike. I could do this with my own dataset if I have to, but I'd prefer to use all the available data if possible.

I think I understand the basic premise of the "batch" approach, at least from the c++ side. I think I would split the model matrix into smaller matrices using some kind of index, do the matrix * coef multiplication on each matrix separately, then add them to the likelihood at the end. However, I'm unsure of how I'd accomplish this from the R side; are there any examples of others that have managed to do something similar?

Javier -------------------------------------------

The reason that the model runs more quickly when you don't specify k=60 is that the default number of basis dimensions for a 2D GAM is 30 (i.e. k=30).

Thanks,
SR

